## Your VPS question — answered directly

**Yes. Phases 2, 3, and 4 run entirely on the VPS.** All those pages are
public (Aldi, shop websites, FB business pages, Google Places). No
logged-in session, no Akamai, no 2FA, no real-browser fingerprinting needed.
The VPS can fetch, scrape, OCR flyers, and match against your shopping list
(the sheet is already VPS-accessible) all on its own.

**The local PC is needed for exactly one thing: WW + Coles.** They're the
only stores that lock down with Akamai + login + 2FA. Everything else is
open.

So the architecture splits cleanly:

```
LOCAL PC (SikuliX/pyautogui)        VPS (Claw brain + scrapers)
┌──────────────────────────┐        ┌─────────────────────────────┐
│ WW + Coles logged-in      │  chat  │ Sheet read/write             │
│ flows: read lists,        │◄──────►│ Wednesday compare pipeline   │
│ add items to lists        │ ntfy/  │ Aldi monthly scrape          │
│ (2FA prompt relayed)      │  TG    │ Mall/shop location search    │
└──────────────────────────┘        │ FB flyer download + OCR      │
                                     │ LLM: "Oranges on special at   │
                                     │  Fruit World, 2 steps from WW"│
                                     └─────────────────────────────┘
```

The two example outputs you gave are pure VPS+LLM work:
- *"Oranges on super special at Fruit World, 2 steps from Woolworths"* ←
  VPS scrapes Fruit World's specials, matches "oranges" against your list,
  knows Fruit World is near WW (Google Places proximity), Claw writes the
  sentence.
- *"Beef stir fry on special at Marjaan butchery, walk over and save $$$"* ←
  same pattern: VPS scrapes/OCRs the butcher's flyer, matches, Claw writes.

None of that touches your local PC.

